          <tr><td>4</td><td>Cameron <strong>(new)</strong></td><td>27</td><td>8.53 GW</td><td>+2.77 GW</td><td>Gas</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>Wilbarger</td><td>21</td><td>8.45 GW</td><td>−0.06 GW</td><td>Gas</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>Reeves</td><td>22</td><td>8.33 GW</td><td>No change</td><td>Gas</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>Milam</td><td>29</td><td>8.31 GW</td><td>+0.02 GW</td><td>Solar</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8</td><td>Jack</td><td>17</td><td>7.98 GW</td><td>No change</td><td>Battery</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9</td><td>Hill</td><td>22</td><td>7.78 GW</td><td>+0.02 GW</td><td>Gas</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10</td><td>Mitchell</td><td>17</td><td>7.50 GW</td><td>+0.71 GW</td><td>Battery</td></tr>
          <tr><td>11</td><td>Freestone <strong>(new)</strong></td><td>24</td><td>7.34 GW</td><td>+1.98 GW</td><td>Gas</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12</td><td>San Patricio</td><td>26</td><td>6.65 GW</td><td>No change</td><td>Battery</td></tr>
          <tr><td>13</td><td>Harris</td><td>28</td><td>6.52 GW</td><td>−0.31 GW</td><td>Battery</td></tr>
          <tr><td>14</td><td>Deaf Smith</td><td>11</td><td>6.39 GW</td><td>+0.01 GW</td><td>Solar</td></tr>
          <tr><td>15</td><td>Fisher</td><td>13</td><td>6.38 GW</td><td>−0.01 GW</td><td>Gas</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>The biggest movers</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cameron County</strong> (+2.77 GW) went from batteries to gas as its largest fuel, on the strength of the three Rattler units. It now has about 4.5 GW of gas queued.</li>
        <li><strong>Callahan County</strong> (+2.23 GW) climbed from #94 to #39 with the two Ironwood gas projects.</li>
        <li><strong>Freestone County</strong> (+1.98 GW) and <strong>Leon County</strong> (+1.83 GW) each added two Black Mountain units. Leon jumped from #33 to #18.</li>
        <li><strong>Bell County</strong> (+0.94 GW) added the 1,140 MW TempleCloud Gas project.</li>
        <li><strong>Refugio County</strong> (−1.64 GW) fell the furthest after Ross Solar and Ross Storage left the list.</li>
        <li><strong>Falls County</strong> (−0.90 GW) lost Roseland Solar I and II, which ERCOT had listed as commissioned. Projects leave the report for different reasons, including finishing construction or withdrawing, and the report doesn't say which.</li>
        <li><strong>Llano County</strong> is new to the map with a single 201 MW battery project, bringing the total to 193 counties.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>⚠️ Reading One Month of Data</h2>
      <p>One month is a short window, and queue filings are cheap compared with building a power plant. The biggest new gas projects don't have interconnection agreements yet, and many queued projects never get built. But a single developer filing seven gigawatt-scale gas units in one month is a signal worth watching, especially in counties like Cameron, Leon and Freestone that may not be expecting it. If one of these counties is yours, our <a href="/guides/data-center-near-you/">Data Center Near You guide</a> explains how to find the TCEQ air permits and local agenda items that usually follow.</p>

      <div class="queue-download" id="download">
